## Context

Split from [[1702]], which had grown to about 40 exit criteria across M2,
M3a and M3b. This child holds the storage targets that M2 needs: the tables
and columns that the extraction pipeline writes when it reads every held
document (`docs/jetp-extraction.md` § 13, the M2 slice). The normative text
is the accepted specification v1 (tag `jetp-spec-v1`): storage contract
`docs/jetp-ledger-storage.md` § 1 (target schema) and § 4, extraction §§ 3,
5, 6, 7, 8, 9, operation § 5. Criteria keep their review finding codes
(W1-, W2-, X-) from tickets 1710 and 1711.

Sibling slices: [[1871]] (M3a collection tables) and [[1872]] (M3b
matching, observations and assets). The M4 target (`document-addresses`,
W1-51) stays on the 1702 tracker.

[[1506]] (pipeline core) is blocked by this ticket: its writer must target
these journals, not `lines.d/` and a dispositions file alone.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] The in-force rule keeps an accepted decision in force while a later revision is only proposed; a red test replays a candidate superseding an accepted row
- [ ] A judgement row (document identity judgements included, written at M2 by [[1501]]) records a stance, an IPCC likelihood term and an IPCC confidence level; existing `confidence` values are mapped by a stated, reviewed rule
- [ ] A statement (line) records the method, method version and reviewer (checking reader, and the author where they decided) that read it, as `docs/jetp-extraction.md` § 3 requires
- [ ] The statement identifier scheme says how ordinals continue across snapshots of one document, so a new snapshot's statements get new identifiers without collision (`docs/jetp-extraction.md` § 9)
- [ ] The vocabulary pass's proposed rename of the row status `candidate` to `proposed` is decided (its verifier advised against it), and applied if accepted (ticket 1703)
- [ ] `line-referents` and `relations` carry `recorded_at` (ledger write time) beside `decided_at`, and a DDL test replays the as-of case of the storage contract § 1: A accepted, B superseding A recorded after K, the state at K returns A (W1-06)
- [ ] `line-referents` and `relations` carry `basis` (the quoted basis) beside stance, likelihood and confidence, and `adjudications` carries `method`, `method_version`, stance, likelihood, confidence, basis and a justification (W1-08)
- [ ] `line-referents.justification_line_ids` is replaced by rows of a relation table, one per decision row and justification line (W1-08)
- [ ] `line-field-specs` is keyed by document and table segment and maps each printed header, with its unit and scale wording, to a field name under a versioned mapping, naming the document type or series the list was copied from (W1-13, W2-03)
- [ ] `lines.classification` is NOT NULL in the DDL (W1-40)
- [ ] `documents.edition_of` is dropped; the `relations` row is the one home of an edition relation (W1-44)
- [ ] The writer splits an oversized `line-fields/<document_id>` into numbered `.d/NN.csv` shards, and a living document's fields table by year of `recorded_at` (W1-14, W1-53)
- [ ] The validator checks prose locators as start and end anchors derived from the verbatim quote (extraction § 5) on lines admitted after the rule, and accepts `text` as the table segment of a prose line identifier (W1-02, W1-09)
- [ ] `lines` carries `run_id`, `method` and `method_version`; `observations`, `line-referents`, `relations` and `adjudications` carry `run_id` beside their method columns; every `run_id` names a `runs` row (W1-01)
- [ ] A `readings` table, append-only under the in-force rule, holds one row per reader proposal, arbitration and author decision (roles `reader`, `second_reader`, `arbiter`, `author`), rejected and undetermined proposals included, with the fields of the storage contract § 1 (target journals), tokens, cost and GPU seconds among them, sharded by run; the validator checks that every assisted-reading line has a row from each reader that proposed it, and an `arbiter` row unless the two readers agreed (W1-01, W2-12, W2-21, W2-23)
- [ ] A `runs` table, append-only, holds one row per run, written at its start with the state `running` and superseded at its end by the final state; a `running` row whose session is gone is closed as `failed`; no list column (W1-01, W2-11)
- [ ] `lines` carries `status`, `supersedes`, `hidden` and `adapter_version`; a DDL test shows a superseded line absent from the in-force view and present in the as-of state before its supersession; counts read in-force lines only (W1-01, W2-01)
- [ ] A `dispositions` table, append-only under the in-force rule, holds one row per disposition with the fields of the storage contract § 1, its kind a `terms` row (the eight kinds of extraction § 7), and the validator checks the kind's grain against whether a snapshot is named (W1-01, W2-01)
- [ ] A `restatements` table, append-only, holds line-to-origin and whole-snapshot restatements with the adapter version; the validator refuses a restatement whose origin is itself a restatement (W2-02)
- [ ] The closed lists of the journals are `terms` rows: extraction method, reading role, stance, outcome, step at fault, run state, `hosted_reading` (W2-22)
- [ ] The locator syntax covers a record with no publisher identifier (ordinal and hash of its canonical serialisation) and a transcribed region (page index and page-normalised rectangle) (W2-09, W2-14)
- [ ] Full raw model responses are stored under their hash in the DVC document store, beside the document bytes, and each `readings` row points to its response by hash (W1-01)
- [ ] `retrievals` carries `terms_position` (with the value processing reserved), `robots_position` and `registration_used`, and `documents` carries `access_route_kind` and `registration`; the validator requires a route kind on every held document (W1-29, W2-10, W2-20; the non-empty rule for the rest is [[1871]])
- [ ] The validator accepts an assertion index in a prose locator and checks (`sha256`, `locator`) uniqueness with it, so several statements on one span are distinct (X-03)
- [ ] `lines` carries a nullable `attributed_party_id`, a foreign key into `parties`, empty meaning the document's publishers jointly (X-04)
- [ ] A `line-groups` relation table (line_id, heading_line_id) replaces `lines.groups`, so a line can sit under several headings and method notes (X-22)
- [ ] `documents` carries `hosted_reading` (`allowed`, `local_only`), and no hosted call is made for a `local_only` document; each `runs` row records the endpoint and the data-collection and retention settings of its hosted calls (X-07, W2-20; setting the column from `terms_position` by rule is [[1871]])
- [ ] The storage contract § 1 and § 4 describe what the code does for the tables of this slice; the migration leaves the lines of the 254 extracted documents byte-identical or explains each change
- [ ] `make jetp-ledger-check`, `make check-fast`, `make lint` and `make check-domain-jetp` pass
